1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,988
Open your form trigger, unpin the test data, and click Publish in the top right of

2
00:00:04,988 --> 00:00:05,611
the canvas.

3
00:00:05,611 --> 00:00:10,249
That turns your Test URL into a real, standing form: one you can bookmark and open

4
00:00:10,249 --> 00:00:12,342
after every client call from here on.

5
00:00:12,342 --> 00:00:14,435
Nothing else about the build changes.

6
00:00:14,435 --> 00:00:18,790
The prompt, the model, the ceiling from your job description, all of it stays

7
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:20,204
exactly as you tested it.

8
00:00:20,204 --> 00:00:24,786
Then make the ritual real, because an agent that requires remembering is an agent

9
00:00:24,786 --> 00:00:26,879
that stops being used in a fortnight.

10
00:00:26,879 --> 00:00:31,574
Put the form's URL where the end of a call will actually meet it: a bookmark on the

11
00:00:31,574 --> 00:00:36,042
browser bar, or a link in the calendar entry template for your recurring calls.

12
00:00:36,042 --> 00:00:40,285
Priya's version takes under a minute: call ends, transcript copied, pasted,

13
00:00:40,285 --> 00:00:44,867
submitted, and the action list is waiting by the time she has refilled her water.

14
00:00:44,867 --> 00:00:49,448
The twenty minute write up from lecture 4.1 is now a sixty second errand plus the

15
00:00:49,448 --> 00:00:53,351
checking, and the checking is the part that was always worth a human.

16
00:00:53,901 --> 00:00:58,186
On the trial length, three or four calls is the whole trial, and the smallness is

17
00:00:58,186 --> 00:01:02,524
deliberate: the unit of evidence is a call, each one hand checked line by line, so

18
00:01:02,524 --> 00:01:04,163
four calls is four full audits.

19
00:01:04,163 --> 00:01:08,395
If all four came back clean against your memory, you have better evidence than a

20
00:01:08,395 --> 00:01:10,141
month of skimming would give you.

21
00:01:10,141 --> 00:01:12,257
The last row matters more than it looks.

22
00:01:12,257 --> 00:01:13,526
A form is easy to share.

23
00:01:13,526 --> 00:01:17,969
Section 10 is where sharing becomes a deliberate rollout instead of an accident, and

24
00:01:17,969 --> 00:01:20,932
this is the moment worth remembering when you get there.

25
00:01:20,932 --> 00:01:24,846
Why not just send the link around now, if colleagues would find it useful?

26
00:01:24,846 --> 00:01:27,861
Because a shared form quietly changes what the system is.

27
00:01:27,861 --> 00:01:32,252
Every transcript submitted through it runs on your model credential, under the data

28
00:01:32,252 --> 00:01:36,589
terms you accepted, through the briefing you tuned for your calls, and none of the

29
00:01:36,589 --> 00:01:39,340
people pasting transcripts in have read any of that.

30
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:43,572
What feels like generosity is actually them inheriting your decisions invisibly.

31
00:01:43,572 --> 00:01:47,750
The rollout section exists to do that handover properly: their own credentials,

32
00:01:47,750 --> 00:01:50,342
their own copy to tune, and a trial of their own.

33
00:01:50,342 --> 00:01:54,521
Until then, the form stays yours, and "who else might use this" stays a list on

34
00:01:54,521 --> 00:01:54,839
paper.

35
00:01:55,389 --> 00:01:57,071
That consistency is deliberate.

36
00:01:57,071 --> 00:02:01,574
Every agent in this course exports the same way, so once you have done it once, you

37
00:02:01,574 --> 00:02:02,985
have done it for all four.

38
00:02:02,985 --> 00:02:07,379
It also means the file itself is genuinely safe to forward: nothing inside it can

39
00:02:07,379 --> 00:02:11,666
leak into a colleague's account by accident, because nothing sensitive was ever

40
00:02:11,666 --> 00:02:13,402
stored in it in the first place.

41
00:02:13,402 --> 00:02:14,975
Safe on credentials, that is.

42
00:02:14,975 --> 00:02:19,479
The Section 3 caveat still applies, and it applies more here: your briefing travels

43
00:02:19,479 --> 00:02:23,114
in the file, and this briefing names Priya and describes her calls.

44
00:02:23,114 --> 00:02:25,230
Yours will name you and describe yours.

45
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:29,733
Glance through the prompt before forwarding, date the filename, and file it next to

46
00:02:29,733 --> 00:02:33,260
C01's export in whatever deliberate place you chose last section.

47
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:35,050
Two templates in that folder now.

48
00:02:35,050 --> 00:02:38,903
By the course's end there will be four, and that folder is the rollout.

49
00:02:39,453 --> 00:02:43,977
Take stock of the pattern before the next build, because it is now a pattern.

50
00:02:43,977 --> 00:02:48,208
Job description, trigger, briefing, pinned test, live checklist, export.

51
00:02:48,208 --> 00:02:53,027
Agent 2 took you one sitting because every step was a smaller version of something

52
00:02:53,027 --> 00:02:57,904
Section 3 already taught, and Agents 3 and 4 will bend the same sequence around new

53
00:02:57,904 --> 00:02:59,667
shapes rather than replace it.

54
00:02:59,667 --> 00:03:04,427
That is what a method feels like from inside: less novelty each time, on purpose.

55
00:03:04,427 --> 00:03:06,014
Two agents down, two to go.

56
00:03:06,014 --> 00:03:10,950
Next section builds the one Priya's director actually reads every Monday: the weekly

57
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:11,361
report.
